Ok...here's a "simple" way to have whetstones work.

1)  They can enchant any item up to the magic level of the stone.  Thus
    a +0 whetstone can resharpen a corroded weapon back to +0.  A +3 whetstone
    is good to +3.  All whetstones enchant one class at a time.  To get
    to +3 (as above) would require 3 seperate uses.

2)  There is a chance of incorrect whetstone usage.  Doing so will either
    ruin the stone (90% chance) or ruin the weapon (10% chance).  Knights
    (because of their weapon/armor training) should have these odds reduced.

3)  Sharpening a weapon takes a *long* time.  Perhaps basing the time to 
    use the whetstone on the weight of the weapon would be the best way
    (it doesn't take as long to sharpen a dagger as a broadsword).

4)  Only bladed weapons could be sharpened.  This makes sense, but if there's
    no seperate category for "bladed" then it's another thing that has to be
    added, at least internally.

5)  The + of the whetstone decreases by one each time it's used; a whetstone
    has no charges.  This is necessary to help preserve game balance.

6)  A blessed whetstone works *much* faster, and anyone can use it.  Even 
    a blessed +0 whetstone would be considered an item to have by many people.

That's about it.  These ideas are just a framework off the top of my head.
I don't feel it's necessary to add sharp/dull; the weapon bonus should be
enough.  And a +0 whetstone would be a great boon to many low-level characters.

